# Where a head's members stand, when they are not indented right below it,
# and what is told of them: a `record`'s fields, a `variant`'s cases and an
# `extern` group's signatures (design.md §4.17; defects 130 and 131,
# 2026-09-29).
#
# Moved out of `parse/records.hero` on 2026-09-29 (lane X4), when a record
# or a variant nested in a block of declarations under a refused head came to
# be read as a top-level one is (`parse/heads.next`): that reader sits below
# the grammar, and `parse/records.hero` reads the grammar for the `:` habit,
# so the questions it shared moved into this leaf, which reads none.
#
# Over members that are there, indented below a head whose line went on, the
# head's junk is the one report, and the members are read; with none, the
# junk and the head's own "empty" report both are, unless the junk is the
# members, written on the head's line. **In braces** the members are read too,
# through the view `parse/braced_lines` lays out (lane X1's finding 1): until
# 2026-09-29 a braced record, variant or group went whole, `y i64` inside it
# unsaid. The braces and the `,` between members are the habit's debris, and
# a `{` is one report where it stands (`no_members_below`).

use token
use cursor
use ast
use diag
use text_lines
use parse/member_lines
use parse/swap_reading
use parse/brace_habit
use parse/head_lines

## Where the members stand

# Where a head's members stand when they are not indented right below it.
variant Below
    # Indented below the head's line, once what goes on past it is dropped.
    indented
    # In braces, their `{` under the cursor and their `}` at `close`, read
    # through the view `parse/braced_lines` lays out.
    braced
        close: i64
    # Nowhere.
    none

# What a head that owes members says where none is indented below it, and
# where they stand once its line is dropped. Over members that are there,
# indented or in braces, the head's junk is the one report; with nothing
# below, `empty` too, the caller's own words and caret; and on the head's own
# line a word the lexer refused is its report already. Shared by a `record`,
# a `variant` and an `extern` group, whose members the caller reads.
#
# **Braces are one report per `{`** (`parse/braces.hero`): a `{` that ends
# the head's line, right after it, is the head's junk, and one past junk
# already told, or alone on the line below a head whose line ended, Allman's
# style, is `empty`'s code where it stands, saying where the members go. Its
# caret is on the `{`'s own line, which its message names: it read *expected
# the end of the line after `record P`* on the line after the one that had
# ended (lane X1's finding 12, 2026-09-29).
function no_members_below(@c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, head: token.Span, members: str, empty: diag.Diagnostic) -> Below
    junk = cursor.current_span(c)
    found = swap_reading.found(c, text)
    spoke = swap_reading.at_reported_error(c, text)
    goes_on = !cursor.at_fresh_line(c)
    generic = cursor.at(c, .lt)
    brace = opening_brace(c, text, goes_on: goes_on)
    quoted = on_one_line(c, text, head: head)

    if brace >= 0
        if goes_on && !spoke
            junk_after_head(@c, head: quoted, found: found, span: junk, members: members, generic: generic)

        while c.pos < brace
            _ = cursor.bump(@c)

        if !goes_on || junk.start != cursor.current_span(c).start
            cursor.say(
                @c,
                diag.new_diagnostic(code: empty.code, message: "the " + members + " of `" + quoted + "` go on the lines below it, one level deeper, and never in braces (found `{`)", span: cursor.current_span(c))
            )
        close = brace_habit.closer(c, open: brace)

        # Braces that hold nothing hold no member either, which is a mistake
        # the braces' report does not name.
        if holds_nothing(c, from: brace, to: close)
            cursor.say(@c, empty)
        return .braced(close: close)

    # The junk is told over members and over none, a closer one too many
    # past it too, and `empty` beside it where none stand (defect 198):
    # `record P )` was told `empty_record` alone and its `)` once fields were
    # written. Where the lexer spoke on the head's line its word is told, and
    # the head with no members lacks them as any does (defect 133's class,
    # lane recovery-b7): `record P fn` was told the `fn` alone. Where the junk
    # is the members, `empty` is its debris (`members_on_its_line`).
    if !spoke && goes_on
        junk_after_head(@c, head: quoted, found: found, span: junk, members: members, generic: generic)
    member = goes_on && !spoke && members_on_its_line(c)

    if goes_on
        brace_habit.drop_told(@c, text, from: c.pos)

    if !cursor.at(c, .indent) && !member
        cursor.say(@c, empty)

    if cursor.at(c, .indent)
        return .indented
    return .none

# The `{` that opens a head's members in braces, or -1: one right after the
# head, where no map can stand, whether its line ends there or its members
# follow it on the line, `record P { x: i64 }`; else the first on the rest of
# the head's line that ends it, or one alone on the line below, where the
# cursor stands once the head's line has ended (`parse/brace_habit`).
function opening_brace(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, goes_on: bool) -> i64
    if goes_on && cursor.at(c, .lbrace)
        return c.pos

    if goes_on
        return brace_habit.ending(c, text)

    if cursor.at(c, .lbrace) && brace_habit.alone(text, cursor.current_span(c))
        return c.pos
    return -1

# Whether the junk from the cursor is the members, written on the head's line
# and sent below by its report: past any type parameters or `extends`, a `:`
# or `=` and a name, or a name and a `:`, `variant T: a`, `record R x: i64`,
# `record Pair<T>: x: T`, `record R = x: i64` (defect 315).
function members_on_its_line(c: cursor.Cursor) -> bool
    i: i64 @ c.pos

    while !token.ends_a_line(c.tokens[i].kind)
        k = c.tokens[i].kind

        if (k == .colon || k == .eq) && c.tokens[i + 1].kind == .ident || k == .ident && c.tokens[i + 1].kind == .colon
            return true
        i @ i + 1

    return false

# Whether nothing but line ends and comments stands between the tokens at
# `from` and `to`.
function holds_nothing(c: cursor.Cursor, from: i64, to: i64) -> bool
    i: i64 @ from + 1

    while i < to
        if !cursor.is_invisible(c.tokens[i].kind)
            return false
        i @ i + 1

    return true

# Where members stand under a head begun at token `from` that failed, as
# `here` finds them; where none stand, `empty` told, the members missing as
# under any head (defect 133's class, lane recovery-b7): `record` alone was
# told its missing name, and its missing fields only once it had one. Not
# where the head's recovery crossed a line, `record = {` over its fields,
# whose braces went with the line: there they are, if not where they go.
function here_or_told(@c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, from: i64, empty: diag.Diagnostic) -> Below
    below = here(c, text)

    nothing = match below
        .none     => true
        .indented => false
        .braced _ => false

    if nothing && !head_lines.crossed_a_line(@c, text, from: from)
        cursor.say(@c, empty)
    return below

# Where members stand right under the cursor: at an indent, or in braces
# that end the line.
function here(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str) -> Below
    if cursor.at(c, .indent)
        return .indented

    if brace_habit.opens_here(c, text)
        return .braced(close: brace_habit.closer(c, open: c.pos))
    return .none

# The fields below a head, where `below` says they stand.
function fields_below(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, below: Below) -> [ast.Field]
    close = close_of(below)
    none: [ast.Field] = []

    if close < 0 && !cursor.at(c, .indent)
        return none

    if close < 0
        return member_lines.field_block(@c, @a, text)
    return member_lines.braced_fields(@c, @a, text)

# The cases below a head, where `below` says they stand.
function cases_below(@c: cursor.Cursor, @a: ast.Ast, text: str, below: Below) -> [ast.Case]
    close = close_of(below)
    none: [ast.Case] = []

    if close < 0 && !cursor.at(c, .indent)
        return none

    if close < 0
        return member_lines.case_block(@c, @a, text)
    return member_lines.braced_cases(@c, @a, text)

# The `}` of members in braces, or -1.
function close_of(below: Below) -> i64
    return match below
        .braced b => b.close
        .indented => -1
        .none     => -1

# A head whose line goes on over the members below it: the junk is the one
# mistake, so it is the one report, and the members are read as they stand.
# `expected_end_of_line` is the code a statement's line gets for the same
# mistake, and the message says where the members go; and where the junk is
# a `<`, `record Pair<T>`, that type parameters are a function's alone (spec
# § 9; defect 135's class, lane recovery-b3).
function junk_after_head(@c: cursor.Cursor, head: str, found: str, span: token.Span, members: str, generic: bool)
    rule: str @ ""

    if generic
        rule @ "type parameters are written on a function only; "
    cursor.error(
        @c,
        code: "expected_end_of_line",
        message: "expected the end of the line after `" + head + "`, found " + found + " — " + rule + "its " + members + " go on the lines below it, one level deeper",
        span: span
    )

# The head as it stands on one line: its tokens as the text spells them, one
# space where a line break stood between two of them, and no comment. The
# lexer joins `record` over `x: i64`, the control arm, `check --permissive`,
# reads the join as the program, and the message quoted the head with the
# break and the margin inside its backticks, `record` + a line end + `x`
# (defect 131's class, 2026-09-29).
function on_one_line(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, head: token.Span) -> str
    i: i64 @ c.pos

    while i > 0 && c.tokens[i - 1].span.start >= head.start
        i @ i - 1

    parts: [str] @ []
    last: i64 @ -1

    while i < c.tokens.len() && c.tokens[i].span.end <= head.end
        t = c.tokens[i]

        if !cursor.is_invisible(t.kind) && t.kind != .eof
            if last >= 0 && text_lines.newlines_between(text, from: last, to: t.span.start) > 0
                parts @ parts.push(" ")
            else if last >= 0
                parts @ parts.push(text.slice(from: last, to: t.span.start))
            parts @ parts.push(cursor.slice_of(text, t.span))
            last @ t.span.end
        i @ i + 1

    return parts.join("")

# What `empty_record` says. Three messages rather than one, because the repair
# differs and §4.17 asks a diagnostic to carry it: outside a group there is no
# header, so fields are the only answer; inside one, a `tag` and no fields is the
# handle form; and a `partial` record with no fields names nothing at all, which
# is the case a reader reaches by deleting a field block and forgetting the word.
function empty_record_message(in_group: bool, partial: bool) -> str
    if !in_group
        return "a `record` needs at least one field, indented one level below it"

    if partial
        return "`partial` names only SOME of the header's fields, so a `partial` `record` with none names nothing — give it its fields, or drop `partial` and name the type for a HANDLE: `record Db tag sqlite3` is C's `sqlite3 *`"
    return "a `record` needs at least one field, indented one level below it — or, in an `extern` group, a `tag` and no fields make it a HANDLE, a pointer to a type the header leaves opaque: `record Db tag sqlite3` is C's `sqlite3 *`"

# `empty_record` at the head it names, in its words (`empty_record_message`).
function empty_record(head: token.Span, in_group: bool, partial: bool) -> diag.Diagnostic
    return diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "empty_record",
        message: empty_record_message(in_group: in_group, partial: partial),
        span: head
    )

# What `empty_variant` says, at its own head `variant Token`, as
# `empty_record` points at its head: it pointed at the next line's first
# token, a line the author did nothing wrong on, until 2026-09-29 (defect
# 131's class).
function empty_variant(written: token.Span) -> diag.Diagnostic
    return diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "empty_variant",
        message: "a `variant` needs at least one case, indented one level below it",
        span: written
    )
